      "id": 1,
      "text": "Loomis first opened its post office in 1889, marking the town’s formal arrival on the map.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loomis%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors J. Loomis, a pioneer who helped establish the local mining community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loomis%2C_Washington",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Loomis grew into a bustling silver boomtown in the 1890s, drawing miners, saloons, and dance halls to its Main Street.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/loomis-washington-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Today, 25,000 acres around Loomis are protected for conservation while guided tours still showcase its mining relics.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/loomis-washington-ghost-town/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Historic remnants in Loomis include ore car tracks, miners’ cabins, and deteriorating Main Street buildings that echo its mining past.",
